              <brickList>
                <brick type="NoteBrick">
                  <brickId>7296e459-edaa-45c6-bfb4-78852c791fc9</brickId>
                  <commentedOut>false</commentedOut>
                  <formulaList>
                    <formula category="NOTE">
                      <additionalChildren/>
                      <type>STRING</type>
                      <value>There seems to be a certain deviation between the time it takes an TemporalAction (like Wait, SetTransparency, etc.) to complete and the actual time which has passed.
This makes the usage of the TIMER-sensor flaky and less useful for testing in CLTs. Timing deviation also appears to be bigger right at the start of a program/script, when we want to measure smaller time intervals and is also different across different devices/emulators.
To counter this flakiness calibration is used. It measures the average deviation of the timer from the intended waiting time (e.g. 1 second) used in the Wait-block, which will be considered as tolerance later in the assert-statements.
Additionally it has been observed that the calibration script itself stabilizes the timer and reduces the timing differences since they seem to get smaller the longer the program has been running.</value>
                    </formula>
                  </formulaList>
                </brick>
